Charnelbop
Ingredients: HARD BOP + DARK, SINISTER & GOTHIC + Cymbals
Creative Catalyst: The genre was born at an illegal rooftop rave eighty stories above a drowned megacity during the torrential storm-surges of 2091.

Charnelbop sounds like an athletic, sweat-drenched jazz sextet playing for their lives inside a flooded gothic cathedral while the tides rise. Blisteringly fast hard bop swing rhythms collide with abyssal pipe-organ pedal drones, minor-key brass laments, and an overwhelming, tactile onslaught of rusted, bowed, and water-dipped Turkish cymbals that sizzle and crash like waves breaking against iron girders.

Genre DNA
* Tempo & Pulse: 180–260 BPM. A frantic, hyper-caffeinated swing drive dominated by a relentless, syncopated ride cymbal pulse ("ding-spang-a-lang") that constantly stumbles into lurching half-time gothic breakdown stomps.
 Harmonic Palette: Dark Aeolian and Phrygian dominant modes colliding with angular hard-bop minor blues scales; dense tritone substitutions voiced on decrepit reed organs to mimic medieval dies irae* chants.
* Production Signatures: 
  1. Cavitation Cymbal Capture: Microphones submerged in tubs of saltwater placed directly beneath hammered bronze cymbals, capturing high-velocity splashes, metallic boiling transients, and eerie pitch-bends.
  2. Crypt-Plate Ambience: Slapback tape delays run through modeled stone mausoleum reverbs with extreme low-end dampening, making horn solos sound stranded in a vast, empty nave.
  3. Iron-Mute Rasp: Saxophones and trumpets played into rusted, corroded metal blast mutes, generating caustic, razor-sharp overtones that cut through heavy bass frequencies.
* Dynamics: Eerily bipolar; tracks typically open with shivering, bowed-cymbal friction and spectral bass murmurs before erupting into a violent, virtuoso bebop sprint, dropping without warning into suffocating, reverbed chant-breaks.

Scene & Origin Lore
In 2091, after the seawalls failed and turned lower Manhattan into an archipelago of stagnant black canals, squatters colonized the upper tiers of half-submerged skyscrapers. Charnelbop crystallized on the roof of Spire 88 during an unlicensed solstice wake. Musicians hauled grand pianos, brass horns, and dozens of cracked cymbals up emergency fire stairs to outplay the roar of military hydrofoils and acid squalls below. The subculture—known to locals as The Silt-Gentry—wears salt-bleached mourning coats, diver hoods, and mourning veils made of shredded wire mesh. Their central ritual is the "Bronze Baptism": during the peak of an improvisational solo, the drummer douses red-hot cymbals into buckets of murky floodwater, releasing clouds of bitter steam over dancers who lindy-hop inches from sheer, seventy-story drops.

Central Instruments: Cracked 24-inch Turkish ride cymbals (bowed, struck with iron rods, and salt-dipped), riveted sizzle cymbals strung with lead wire, spit-valved tenor saxophone, pneumatic-muted trumpet, gut-strung double bass played with an iron bow, and a foot-pumped Victorian field harmonium.

For Fans Of: Art Blakey & The Jazz Messengers, Bauhaus, Colin Stetson, Diamanda Galás.

